      ],
      "updated": 1753207303220,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "At least Three Consecutive Elements \nmust be considered",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "At least Three Consecutive Elements \nmust be considered",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "IsPMwxDUoXwG6MTd8MEI4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 262.21303436781477,
      "y": 438.25002035582884,
      "width": 1773.0230712890625,
      "height": 467.2818335099538,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bMo",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 624670617,
      "version": 1848,
      "versionNonce": 1784839257,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1753208027155,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basic Sliding window approach. The only difference in this question is coming up with a formula to actually count \nthe number of valid Subarrays Satisfying the Above Constraints. \n\nAlgorithm \n0] Initialize both left and right pointers ; Right to keep track of subarrays satisfying the mentioned rule \n   and left pointer to update and check the next possible difference \n1] if nums.length <3 return 0\n2] Else we take the initial difference of both the starting elements diff=n[1]-n[0];\n3] Outer Loop till while(j<n) we have to check for a given diff is there exists subarray with len>=3\n4] So for the given difference count the len of the subarray \n5] update the count variable using the formula to compute the possible number of subarrays of size 3 using \n   valid subarray length ==> len\n6] Hence for a given valid length update the count+=(len-2)*(len-1)/2;\n7] The above formula is very basic and counts all possible subarrays using len of valid subarrays for a given difference\n8] After a updating the count we update the left pointer to left=j-1; and take into account new difference for the next possible sequence \n   diff= n[j]-n[j-1];   && i=j-1  && j++;",
      "fontSize": 23.36409167549769,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basic Sliding window approach. The only difference in this question is coming up with a formula to actually count \nthe number of valid Subarrays Satisfying the Above Constraints. \n\nAlgorithm \n0] Initialize both left and right pointers ; Right to keep track of subarrays satisfying the mentioned rule \n   and left pointer to update and check the next possible difference \n1] if nums.length <3 return 0\n2] Else we take the initial difference of both the starting elements diff=n[1]-n[0];\n3] Outer Loop till while(j<n) we have to check for a given diff is there exists subarray with len>=3\n4] So for the given difference count the len of the subarray \n5] update the count variable using the formula to compute the possible number of subarrays of size 3 using \n   valid subarray length ==> len\n6] Hence for a given valid length update the count+=(len-2)*(len-1)/2;\n7] The above formula is very basic and counts all possible subarrays using len of valid subarrays for a given difference\n8] After a updating the count we update the left pointer to left=j-1; and take into account new difference for the next possible sequence \n   diff= n[j]-n[j-1];   && i=j-1  && j++;",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
